          </div>
        </div>

        <nav class="toc-card" aria-label="Table of Contents">
          <h2 class="toc-title">Table of Contents</h2>
          <ul class="toc-list">
            <li><a href="#how-it-works">How the condensation system works and where the moisture goes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#five-reasons">5 key reasons why your dryer is not collecting water in the container</a></li>
            <li><a href="#self-checks">What you can check yourself when there is no water in the dryer reservoir</a></li>
            <li><a href="#conclusion">Conclusion</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <div class="content-grid">
          <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none article-content">
            <p>
              Modern tumble dryers have quickly evolved from luxury appliances into household essentials, especially during cold or rainy months. They eliminate the need for drying racks, save valuable time, and leave clothes soft, fresh, and ready to wear. The process seems simple and fully automated - load the wet laundry, select a program, and remove perfectly dry clothes at the end of the cycle while emptying the collected water from the reservoir. But what happens when this familiar routine is suddenly interrupted?
            </p>
            <p>
              If, after an intensive two-hour drying cycle, your clothes are still damp and the condensation container is completely empty, this is a clear sign that something is wrong. When a dryer is not collecting water, it not only disrupts the drying process but also indicates that moisture is being trapped somewhere within the appliance's internal systems. In this guide, we will take a detailed look at how the moisture collection system works, what commonly causes this issue, and how to determine when professional assistance is required.
            </p>

            <h2 id="how-it-works">How the condensation system works and where the moisture goes</h2>
            <p>
              To understand why there is no water in your dryer's reservoir, it is important to first understand the path air and moisture follow inside the appliance. Whether you own a conventional condenser dryer or a heat pump dryer, the principles of moisture extraction are largely the same.
            </p>
            <p>
              Warm air is circulated through the drum, where it absorbs moisture from the wet laundry. This moisture-laden air then passes through the lint filters and reaches the heat exchanger (condenser), where it is cooled rapidly. As the temperature drops, the moisture condenses into water droplets that collect in a small sump located at the bottom of the appliance.
            </p>
            <p>
              Inside this lower reservoir is a small condensate pump whose job is to move the collected water through narrow hoses and up into the removable water container. If this chain of processes is interrupted at any point, the system stops functioning properly. The water either remains trapped at the bottom of the dryer or fails to condense altogether, remaining as water vapor inside the drum.
            </p>

            <h2 id="five-reasons">5 key reasons why your dryer is not collecting water in the container</h2>
            <figure class="inline-article-image">
              <img src={warningImage} alt="Clean white tumble dryer with lint trap" loading="lazy" />
            </figure>
            <p>
              When the primary symptom is an empty water container despite a completed drying cycle, the underlying fault is typically mechanical or electrical. Below are the five most common causes identified by appliance repair professionals.
            </p>

            <h3>1. A blocked condensate pump or water pathway</h3>
            <p>
              This is by far the most common cause of the problem. Although dryers are equipped with lint filters, fine textile fibers, dust, pet hair, and other debris inevitably bypass them over time. These particles mix with condensed water at the bottom of the appliance and gradually form a thick, sticky sludge.
            </p>
            <p>
              Once this buildup reaches the sump, it can clog the pump impeller or block the narrow hoses that carry water to the reservoir. The pump continues attempting to operate, but the water cannot reach the container. Most modern dryers will display an error code such as E20, F02, or a similar warning, although the exact code varies by manufacturer.
            </p>

            <h3>2. A faulty water level sensor (float switch)</h3>
            <p>
              Located near the condensate pump is a float switch that monitors the water level inside the sump. When enough water accumulates, the float rises and signals the control board to activate the pump. If the float becomes stuck due to lint buildup, dirt, or organic deposits, it remains in the lowered position. The appliance falsely assumes the sump is empty, the pump never starts, and no water reaches the upper container.
            </p>

            <h3>3. A clogged heat exchanger (condenser)</h3>
            <p>
              If the heat exchanger becomes coated with a thick layer of dust and fibers, it loses its ability to cool the warm, humid air passing through it. Instead of releasing its moisture, the air retains it, and the clothes continue tumbling in a constant stream of warm, humid air. As a result, the dryer may overheat, the laundry remains damp, and the water container stays completely dry.
            </p>
            <p>
              A similar issue can occur when the moisture sensors inside the drum become contaminated. Over time, fabric softener residue can accumulate on their metal surfaces, causing them to interpret clothes as drier than they actually are. The dryer then ends the cycle prematurely before enough moisture has condensed and collected.
            </p>

            <h3>4. Wear or failure of the condensate pump</h3>
            <p>
              Like any electrical component, the condensate pump has a limited lifespan. The motor windings can burn out, or the mechanical connection between the motor and impeller can fail. When this happens, the pump simply stops transferring water from the sump to the collection container.
            </p>

            <h3>5. Problems with the heat pump or compressor</h3>
            <p>
              In modern heat pump dryers, both cooling and heating functions rely on refrigerant gases such as R134a or R290 and a compressor system similar to those used in refrigerators. If refrigerant leaks occur or the compressor fails, the appliance can no longer regulate temperatures effectively. Without sufficient cooling, condensation cannot occur. Without condensation, the water container remains empty.
            </p>
            <p class="notice">
              <strong>
                Do not ignore changes in your dryer's performance before trapped moisture causes more serious damage to the appliance's internal electronics. Certified RotoRem technicians provide on-site
