            </div>

            <h2 id="self-checks">What you can check yourself when there is no water in the dryer reservoir</h2>
            <p>
              Before assuming a major fault exists, there are several simple and completely safe checks you can perform yourself.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Check for an external drain connection:</strong> Many modern dryers allow water to be discharged directly into a drain through a hose connected to the rear of the appliance instead of collecting it in the reservoir. If the dryer has recently been moved or reconfigured, verify whether the drainage hose has been connected to a household drain. If it has, it is perfectly normal for the upper water container to remain empty.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Thoroughly clean all filters:</strong> Remove the lint filter located around the door opening and wash it thoroughly using warm water and mild detergent. Fine residues from fabric softeners often clog the microscopic openings in the filter mesh, reducing airflow and affecting condensation efficiency.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Inspect the accessible condenser:</strong> On traditional condenser dryers (without heat pump technology), a service panel is usually located at the lower left side of the appliance. Behind this panel is the metal heat exchanger, which can often be removed completely and rinsed thoroughly under running water to remove accumulated lint and debris.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              If the problem persists after performing these checks, the blockage or malfunction is likely located inside the appliance's housing, where diagnosis requires disassembly and technical expertise.
            </p>
            <p>
              Accumulated water trapped within the lower section of the appliance presents significant technical risks. When the pump fails to move water upward, the water level in the sump continues rising. Eventually, water can overflow beyond the designated channels and spill directly onto the metal base of the dryer.
            </p>
            <p>
              Just as neglected faults in pressurized appliances can create safety hazards, the combination of standing water and electrical components inside a dryer can compromise safe operation. Located near the bottom of the appliance are the main motor, start capacitors, and much of the internal wiring. If water reaches these components, there is a serious risk of short circuits, tripped breakers, or damage to the expensive electronic control board.
            </p>
            <p>
              In addition, constant moisture combined with elevated temperatures creates ideal conditions for mold and mildew growth. Over time, this can produce persistent unpleasant odors that transfer onto otherwise clean laundry.
            </p>
            <p class="notice">
              <strong>
                Timely cleaning of the internal water pathways and removal of accumulated textile fibers helps prevent overheating and protects the condensate pump from failure. You can rely on RotoRem specialists for professional preventive maintenance and
                <a href="https://www.rotorem.bg/en/services/dryer-repair/" class="inline-link">expert dryer servicing throughout Varna</a>, Sofia and the surrounding areas.
              </strong>
            </p>

            <div class="cta-inline" role="group" aria-label="Contact options for dryer repair">
              <a href={callHref} class="cta-btn cta-btn-call">Call: 089 834 0982</a>
              <a href={emailHref} class="cta-btn cta-btn-mail">Send email</a>
            </div>

            <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
            <p>
              If you notice that your dryer has stopped filling its water reservoir, it is a warning sign that should not be ignored. Cleaning accessible filters is an excellent first step in troubleshooting and maintenance. However, if the issue is caused by a blocked pump, a faulty float switch, or a malfunctioning heat pump system, professional intervention is the safest and most effective solution.
            </p>
            <p>
              Proper diagnosis and repair not only extend the lifespan of your appliance but also protect your home from leaks and restore the convenience of perfectly dried laundry.
            </p>

            <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Why does my dryer stop after five minutes and display a full container warning when the reservoir is empty?</h3>
            <p>
              This is a classic symptom of either a stuck float switch in the lower sump or a completely failed condensate pump. Water from a previous drying cycle remains trapped at the bottom of the appliance and activates the safety sensor. Since the dryer detects that it cannot remove additional water, the control software interrupts the cycle to prevent flooding of the motor compartment.
            </p>

            <h3>How can I tell whether the condensate pump is burned out or simply clogged?</h3>
            <p>
              When a pump is clogged with lint and debris, you will often hear a distinct humming, buzzing, or grinding sound coming from the bottom of the appliance as the motor attempts to rotate the blocked impeller. If the pump has failed electrically, it usually remains completely silent while water begins accumulating and potentially leaking beneath the appliance.
            </p>

            <h3>Can excessive use of fabric softener cause this problem?</h3>
            <p>
              Yes. Fabric softeners leave behind a thin, invisible oily film on clothing. During the drying process, this residue evaporates together with moisture and settles on the moisture sensors and heat exchanger surfaces. The resulting layer acts like an adhesive for fine dust particles, dramatically accelerating the formation of the sticky sludge that clogs the dryer's water pathways.
            </p>

            <p class="disclaimer">
              The information provided in this article is intended for informational purposes only and should not be considered a substitute for professional technical diagnostics or consultation with a qualified appliance repair specialist.
            </p>
          </div>
